{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 2350 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "7f6f3b6fd01441522600958d6dd00cab1311dad3410db656c767abf29208c42b" :edb/db-before-t 2349 :edb/db-after-t 2350 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535662 50 #inst "2026-10-09T04:39:20.664Z" 13194139535662 true] [17592186092025 1100 "Find an eigenvector of ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069178/assets/a37420b7df40cfb806fadac008680a2e7346b1b2dea5d89a4baa912ab0bedb14.png)\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\,\\lambda=2.$" 13194139535662 true] [17592186092025 1100 "Find an eigenvector of $\\begin{bmatrix}7 & -4 \\\\ 5 & -2\\end{bmatrix}$\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\,\\lambda=2$." 13194139535662 false] [17592186092025 1145 "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation\n\n$$\n(A - \\lambda I) \\textbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\,\\lambda =2\n$$\n\nSo, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA - λI &= \\begin{bmatrix}7 & -4 \\\\ 5 & -2\\end{bmatrix} - 2\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}5 & -4 \\\\ 5 & -4\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe have to solve the corresponding homogeneous system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{5x_{1} - 4x_{2} = 0; 5x_{1} - 4x_{2} = 0\n$$\n\nSo, we row-reduce the augmented matrix $M$ to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nM &= \\begin{bmatrix}5 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 5 & -4 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\mid R_{2}:=R_{2} + (-1)R_{1} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). So, $x_2$ is a free variable.\n\nFrom the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n5x_1-4x_2=0 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad x_1=\\dfrac{4}{5}x_2\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{4}{5}x_{2} \\\\ x_{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFinally, setting $x_2=5$, we get the eigenvector\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" 13194139535662 false] [17592186092025 1145 "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A - \\lambda I) \\textbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,\\lambda =2.$ So, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}7 & -4 \\\\ 5 & -2\\end{aligned}]-2[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & -4 \\\\ 5 & -4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe have to solve the corresponding homogeneous system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}5{x}_{1}-4{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 5{x}_{1}-4{x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we row-reduce the augmented matrix $M$ to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 5 & -4 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}5 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). So, $x_2$ is a free variable. \n\nFrom the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n5x_1-4x_2=0\n\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\nx_1=\\dfrac{4}{5}x_2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069178/assets/65b8ab3a3e0506afb1a0c3a7a928e21b98bcda313bafcbcfdb5b02771dd91693.png)\n\nFinally, setting $x_2=5,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069178/assets/a5098e5100bed7d15053e81b1127a774b828e30473718c6fc3fe73444eca3148.png)" 13194139535662 true] [17592186092026 1100 "If ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069178/assets/c3f5f7169fd10d5710a95d0eab9c290faa04a544ac646a2ebcd24aa6b7e5f714.png) is an eigenvector of ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069178/assets/69c5e6ae96062d7c8e289e1f0070ab3874b15d0b42bb9300a50b9315723cfd8f.png)\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\,\\lambda=1$, then what is the value of $\\,\\dfrac{b}{a}\\,?$" 13194139535662 true] [17592186092026 1100 "If $\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix}$ is an eigenvector of $\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 5 \\\\ -1 & -4\\end{bmatrix}$\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\,\\lambda=1$, then what is the value of $\\,\\dfrac{b}{a}\\,$?" 13194139535662 false] [17592186092026 1145 "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation\n\n$$\n(A - \\lambda I) \\textbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\,\\lambda =1\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA - λI &= \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 5 \\\\ -1 & -4\\end{bmatrix} - \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 \\\\ -1 & -5\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe have to solve the corresponding homogeneous system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{x_{1} + 5x_{2} = 0; - x_{1} - 5x_{2} = 0\n$$\n\nSo, we row-reduce the augmented matrix $M$ to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nM &= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -5 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\mid R_{2}:=R_{2} + R_{1} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). So, $x_2$ is a free variable.\n\nFrom the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\nx_1+5x_2=0 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad x_1=-5x_2\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-5x_{2} \\\\ x_{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, $x_2=-1$, we get the following eigenvector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{b}{a}=\\dfrac{-1}{5}=-\\dfrac{1}{5}\n$$" 13194139535662 false] [17592186092026 1145 "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A - \\lambda I) \\textbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,\\lambda =1.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 5 \\\\ -1 & -4\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 \\\\ -1 & -5\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe have to solve the corresponding homogeneous system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+5{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ -{x}_{1}-5{x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we row-reduce the augmented matrix $M$ to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -5 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). So, $x_2$ is a free variable. \n\nFrom the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\nx_1+5x_2=0\n\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\nx_1=-5x_2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069178/assets/f8c4aba8e8fb922f08741deb8df0fe4f47c64c29b647fec7da2b0ce1b4367225.png)\n\nSetting, for instance, $x_2=-1,$ we get the following eigenvector:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069178/assets/5f7d229b6636bbfbe8eab4bdb7a6b8e72bb21c9cc9e66b1b4d607fd0e5e352e0.png)\n\nFinally, $\\dfrac{b}{a}=\\dfrac{-1}{5}=-\\dfrac{1}{5}.$" 13194139535662 true] [17592186092027 1100 "Given that $\\lambda=1$ and $\\lambda=-4$ are the eigenvalues of the matrix\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069178/assets/10510533350f1e77e4918cb4aff9edafe5ced005efc845becc8c1558bdd62811.png)\nfind two linearly independent eigenvectors of $A.$" 13194139535662 true] [17592186092027 1100 "Given that $\\lambda=1$ and $\\lambda=-4$ are the eigenvalues of the matrix\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{bmatrix}$\nfind two linearly independent eigenvectors of $A$." 13194139535662 false] [17592186092027 1145 "We can get two linearly independent eigenvectors by simply finding eigenvectors corresponding to the different eigenvalues.\n\nTo compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation\n\n$$\n(A - \\lambda I) \\textbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the eigenvalues separately.\n\n- For $\\lambda =1$, we have\n$A - λI|= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{bmatrix} - 1\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}; = \\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -5\\end{bmatrix}$.\nWe have to solve the corresponding system of linear equations:\n${-x_{2} = 0; - 5x_{2} = 0$\nSo, we row-reduce the augmented matrix $M$ to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n$M|= \\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\mid R_{2}:=R_{2} + (-5)R_{1}; ∼\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $2$nd one). So, $x_1$ is a free variable.\nFrom the first equation, we obtain $x_2=0$. Therefore, the general solution is\n$\\begin{bmatrix}x_{1} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$\nSetting, for instance, $x_1=1$, we get the eigenvector\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$\n\n- For $\\lambda =-4$, we have\n$A - λI|= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{bmatrix} - (-4)\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}; = \\begin{bmatrix}5 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$.\nWe have to solve the corresponding system of linear equations:\n${5x_{1} - x_{2} = 0; 0 = 0$\nSo, we row-reduce the augmented matrix $M$ to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n$M|= \\begin{bmatrix}5 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$\nIn the matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). So, $x_2$ is a free variable.\nFrom the first equation, we obtain $x_1=\\dfrac{1}{5}x_2$. Therefore, the general solution is\n$\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{5}x_{2} \\\\ x_{2}\\end{bmatrix}$\nSetting, for instance, $x_2=5$, we get the eigenvector\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nTherefore, two linearly independent eigenvectors of the matrix $A$ are\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139535662 false] [17592186092027 1145 "We can get two linearly independent eigenvectors by simply finding eigenvectors corresponding to the different eigenvalues.\n\nTo compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A - \\lambda I) \\textbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the eigenvalues separately. \n\n- For $\\lambda =1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{aligned}]-1[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -5\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe have to solve the corresponding system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ -5{x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we row-reduce the augmented matrix $M$ to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-5){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $2$nd one). So, $x_1$ is a free variable. \nFrom the first equation, we obtain  $x_2=0.$ Therefore, the general solution is\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069178/assets/97e9cc73b2d39b924618c1372bf680ebf7d4374d1cf4fa9c3bc7e9afc05b06ea.png)\nSetting, for instance, $x_1=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069178/assets/a01037d3f9d9e090010a555fa0e14d6bce72da48c59bd74b0f7dad6afcee71ba.png)\n\n- For $\\lambda =-4,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{aligned}]-(-4)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe have to solve the corresponding system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}5{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 0=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we row-reduce the augmented matrix $M$ to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). So, $x_2$ is a free variable. \nFrom the first equation, we obtain $x_1=\\dfrac{1}{5}x_2.$ Therefore, the general solution is\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069178/assets/34313b24e58011d8be4790d57cd57d4800944903ff16dd20068b45bab2a7d261.png)\nSetting, for instance, $x_2=5,$ we get the eigenvector \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069178/assets/cb7300ef2064945937c27944e51032ade506ea16008e22f1a6ab54c5c647812c.png)\n\nTherefore, two linearly independent eigenvectors of the matrix $A$ are\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069178/assets/f6638f602a661865b1940f1409a20611c08f38821e2abcb204a4b38dd3a7adaa.png)" 13194139535662 true] [17592186092180 1020 #edb/ref 17592186359834 13194139535662 true] [17592186092180 1020 #edb/ref 17592186359841 13194139535662 true] [17592186092180 1020 #edb/ref 17592186359848 13194139535662 true] [17592186092180 1020 #edb/ref 17592186359876 13194139535662 true] [17592186092180 1020 #edb/ref 17592186359883 13194139535662 true] [17592186092181 1020 #edb/ref 17592186359827 13194139535662 true] [17592186092181 1020 #edb/ref 17592186359862 13194139535662 true] [17592186092181 1020 #edb/ref 17592186359869 13194139535662 true] [17592186092181 1020 #edb/ref 17592186359904 13194139535662 true] [17592186092181 1020 #edb/ref 17592186359911 13194139535662 true] [17592186092182 1020 #edb/ref 17592186359855 13194139535662 true] [17592186092182 1020 #edb/ref 17592186359890 13194139535662 true] [17592186092182 1020 #edb/ref 17592186359897 13194139535662 true] [17592186092182 1020 #edb/ref 17592186359918 13194139535662 true] [17592186092182 1020 #edb/ref 17592186359925 13194139535662 true] [17592186359827 1017 #edb/ref 17592186359828 13194139535662 true] [17592186359827 1089 "q-107109" 13194139535662 true] [17592186359827 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535662 true] [17592186359827 1100 "If $v=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]$ is an eigenvector of \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 20 & 13\\end{aligned}]$\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-2,$\nthen what is the value of $\\frac{b}{a}?$" 13194139535662 true] [17592186359827 1113 #uuid "7139a2cb-9937-5b21-8c2d-93c8866b4221" 13194139535662 true] [17592186359827 1145 "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,\\lambda =-2.$ So, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 20 & 13\\end{aligned}]-(-2)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 \\\\ 20 & 15\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}4{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 20{x}_{1}+15{x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 20 & 15 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-5){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is 